<article class="env-remark" id="04MR">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04MR">Remark <span data-tag="04MR">40.10.11</span>.</a> Warning: Lemma <a href="/tag/04MQ" data-tag="04MQ">40.10.10</a> is wrong without the condition that $s$ and $t$ are locally of finite type. An easy example is to start with the action </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathbf{G}_{m, \mathbf{Q}} \times _{\mathbf{Q}} \mathbf{A}^1_{\mathbf{Q}} \to \mathbf{A}^1_{\mathbf{Q}}  \]
</div>
  <p> and restrict the corresponding groupoid scheme to the generic point of $\mathbf{A}^1_{\mathbf{Q}}$. In other words restrict via the morphism $\mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\mathbf{Q}(x)) \to \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\mathbf{Q}[x]) = \mathbf{A}^1_{\mathbf{Q}}$. Then you get a groupoid scheme $(U, R, s, t, c)$ with $U = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}(\mathbf{Q}(x))$ and </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  R = \mathop{\mathrm{Spec}}\left( \mathbf{Q}(x)[y]\left[ \frac{1}{P(xy)}, P \in \mathbf{Q}[T], P \not= 0 \right] \right)  \]
</div>
  <p> In this case $\dim (R) = 1$ and $\dim (G) = 0$. </p>
</article>